  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nNevada remains under southwesterly flow aloft as a large upper \nlevel trough over coastal Oregon tried to push against the \nsubtropical upper level ridge over the southern plains. This upper\ntrough will slowly push through the Pacific NW and into the \ninter-mountain west through Wednesday. The main impact from this \ntrough will be from the winds as the pressure gradient helps drive\nbreezy conditions across eastern and central Nevada as SW winds \nrise during the afternoons to 15 to 25 MPH with gusts up to 35 MPH\npossible. Winds father west will be slightly lighter at 10 to 20 \nMPH with gust up to 30 MPH possible. The next impacts from this \nsystem will be to keep temperature on the cool side through Monday\nwith high in the mid to upper 70s, before a quick warm-up Tuesday\nand Wednesday get temperatures back into the upper 80s for Late \nweek into the start of the following weekend as the upper trough \ndeparts and the upper ridge tries to shift back west. Models which\nwere once hopeful for precipitation thanks to moisture from \nHurricane Marie which will be well of shore of the Baja Peninsula,\nbut in line with the SW flow aloft. Have kept trending drier as \nany moisture from Marie looks to stay across southern Nevada this \nweek. As for the trough, the best moisture looks to stay north \ninto Oregon and Idaho. Overall that leave a low chance for showers\nand thunderstorms Sunday across far northern Nevada. While \nanother low chance for showers and a few thunderstorms will be \npossible for SE White Pine county with a few storms reaching as \nfar north as the Ruby Mountains. Chances for storms and showers \nlooks to remain between 10% and 30% mainly during the afternoon \nand evening hours Sunday. The next chance of showers or storms \nlooks to be Thursday through Friday as more moisture from Marie \nrides along the periphery of the upper ridge which will be \nbuilding back toward the southwest at this time. After Friday \nModel diverge significantly as the upper pattern looks to return \nto a typical summer type upper ridging, or the possibility of a \nsecond upper trough pushing through the Pacific NW. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence cooler than normal temperatures \ncontinuing through Tuesday. \n\nThere is moderate confidence in a 10% to 30% chance for isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms this afternoon across northern and\neastern portions of the CWA. \n\nThere is low confidence for the long range forecast as model \nsolutions show a high degree of divergence for the upper pattern \nafter Friday.",
